  "text": "I've created `filter.py`. Run it as `python3 filter.py file.html` and it rewrites the file in place with the JavaScript removed. It only deletes the dangerous parts and never rebuilds the HTML, so everything else stays exactly as it was, down to line endings and the original encoding.\n\n**What it removes:**\n- `<script>` and `<iframe>` elements, including everything inside them.\n- Tags that can run or load code: `frame`, `frameset`, `object`, `embed`, `applet`, `param`, `base` and `portal`. The content between `object`/`embed` tags is kept.\n- `<meta http-equiv=\"refresh\">` and `<link rel=\"import\">`.\n- SVG animation tags that target `href` or event attributes.\n- Any attribute starting with `on` (like `onerror` or `onload`), and `srcdoc`.\n- Links and attribute values that use `javascript:`, `vbscript:` and similar, including when they're hidden with entities (`&#106;…`, `&colon;`) or tabs and spaces.\n- `data:` links, except ordinary images (png, jpeg, gif, webp and similar). SVG `data:` images are removed too.\n- `style` attributes and `<style>` blocks containing `expression(`, `javascript:`, `behavior:` or `-moz-binding`.\n\n**How it decides:** it reads tags the same way a browser does. It also checks every place a tag could start, including inside comments, attribute values and `<textarea>`/`<noscript>`/`<style>` blocks. That blocks tricks that depend on the filter and the browser reading the HTML differently, such as `<textarea><a title=\"</textarea><img onerror=…>\">` or `<!--> <img onerror…> -->`.\n\n**Testing:**\n- I ran about 35 attack examples through the filter. Then I loaded 23 of them in headless Chromium, before and after filtering. 19 of the 23 triggered an alert before filtering; none did after. The other four didn't fire even unfiltered, so those runs show nothing either way, and three of them also had no alert hook to catch one.\n- A clean HTML page with tables, headings, a comment, styles, entities, a `data:` image and non-English text came out byte-for-byte identical.\n- A 2.5 MB file took about 5 seconds.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- It leans towards removing too much. For example, a legitimate `javascript:void(0)` link loses its `href`, and HTML written out as plain text inside a `<textarea>` gets cleaned as if it were real tags.\n- When a tag is removed but its content stays, as with `object`/`embed`, the content is still cleaned the same way.",
